{"success": true, "message": "Test details sent successfully", "data": {"test": {"_id": "69de21eb7e39d99b57bc5aae", "analysisVideoCdn": "yt", "exam": ["605dfcb2f226ac25a8bd5fef"], "ctype": null, "position": 0, "order": 12, "enableCalculator": false, "isContinuousQuestion": false, "reattemptAvailable": false, "singleSectionMultipleSubjects": false, "timeRestricted": false, "title": "Gravitation - Test 2", "description": "Previous Year Questions as Chapter Test", "syllabus": "This test contains questions from all topics of this chapter included in the JEE Main syllabus. It includes a mix of questions from PYQs of JEE Main 2019 - 2025.", "totalTime": 50, "totalQuestions": 25, "totalMarks": 100, "bonusQuestions": null, "bonusMarks": null, "startDate": "2026-04-15T03:30:00.000Z", "endDate": "2027-04-30T00:00:00.000Z", "type": "free", "class": "605df635a37b7025c438d685", "instruction": "608289ffb160b022e9442c83", "subjects": [{"subjectId": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "totalMarks": 100, "bonusMarks": 0, "totalQuestions": 25, "bonusQuestions": 0, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e70d"}], "sections": [{"sectionId": {"_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6c7", "title": "Physics Single Correct", "sectionType": "normal", "subject": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "questions": [{"_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e660", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["6117b60a478ae845c43c63f1"], "concepts": ["6117b607478ae845c43c62fb"], "question": {"text": "Two particles of equal mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi></math>&nbsp;move in a circle of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi></math>&nbsp;under the action of their mutual gravitational attraction. The speed of each particle will be :"}, "options": [{"id": "6938f4", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi>G</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e661"}, {"id": "335302", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>G</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></mfrac></msqrt></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e662"}, {"id": "494658", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi>G</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></mfrac></msqrt></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e663"}, {"id": "e2770f", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi>G</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e664"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e665", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e5678e499d1e49588f927a"], "concepts": ["6162faf16ed13040ec849361"], "question": {"text": "The acceleration due to gravity on the surface of earth is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>g</mi></math>. If the diameter of earth reduces to half of its original value and mass remains constant, then acceleration due to gravity on the surface of earth would be :"}, "options": [{"id": "1dca2d", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>g</mi><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e666"}, {"id": "42940e", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e667"}, {"id": "0da947", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e668"}, {"id": "1fbfd8", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mi>g</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e669"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e66a", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b6b"], "concepts": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b2f"], "question": {"text": "A body of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi></math> is projected with velocity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#955;</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></math> in vertically upward direction from the surface of the earth into space. It is given that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></math> is escape velocity and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>&#60;</mo><mn>1</mn></math>. If air resistance is considered to the negligible, then the maximum height from the centre of earth, to which the body can go, will be (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math> : radius of earth)"}, "options": [{"id": "5b686e", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>R</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e66b"}, {"id": "29ae7a", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>R</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>-</mo><msup><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e66c"}, {"id": "c2ebbf", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>R</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>-</mo><mi>&#955;</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e66d"}, {"id": "b822b7", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>R</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>-</mo><msup><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e66e"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e66f", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e5671c499d1e49588f87fd"], "concepts": ["60e5671a499d1e49588f87c5"], "question": {"text": "Two identical particles of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kg</mi></math> each go round a circle of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math>, under the action of their mutual gravitational attraction. The angular speed of each particle is:"}, "options": [{"id": "8ad11b", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mfrac><mi>G</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e670"}, {"id": "262b33", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msqrt><mfrac><mi>G</mi><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mfrac></msqrt></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e671"}, {"id": "a9241f", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>R</mi></mrow></mfrac><msqrt><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>G</mi></mfrac></msqrt></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e672"}, {"id": "b5ca80", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>G</mi></mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mfrac></msqrt></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e673"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e674", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4763"], "concepts": ["60e40fd4f1ef260f84ed1e41"], "question": {"text": "If a satellite orbiting the Earth is 9 times closer to the Earth than the Moon, what is the time period of rotation of the satellite? Given rotational time period of Moon $=27$ days and gravitational attraction between the satellite and the moon is neglected."}, "options": [{"id": "30e9db", "text": "27 days", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e675"}, {"id": "086fae", "text": "1 day", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e676"}, {"id": "79d48d", "text": "81 days", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e677"}, {"id": "43b57a", "text": "3 days", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e678"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e679", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f3e"], "concepts": ["6162faef6ed13040ec849253"], "question": {"text": "A test particle is moving in a circular orbit in the gravitational field produced by a mass density <math><mi>&#961;</mi><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>K</mi></mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></math> Identify the current relation between the radius <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></math> of the particle&rsquo;s orbit and its period <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mo>:</mo></math>"}, "options": [{"id": "560565", "text": "<math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></math> is a constant", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e67a"}, {"id": "01a872", "text": "<math><mfrac bevelled=\"true\"><mrow><msup><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></math> is a constant", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e67b"}, {"id": "1d98b1", "text": "<math><mfrac bevelled=\"true\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow></mfrac></math> is a constant", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e67c"}, {"id": "384b8f", "text": "<math><mfrac bevelled=\"true\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></math> is a constant", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e67d"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e67e", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e5678e499d1e49588f927a"], "concepts": ["60e567fa499d1e49588fa01b"], "question": {"text": "The value of the acceleration due to gravity is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> at a height <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>&nbsp;(<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo></math> radius of the earth) from the surface of the earth. It is again equal to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> at a depth <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>d</mi></math> below the surface the earth. The ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mi>R</mi></mfrac></mfenced></math> equals:"}, "options": [{"id": "c4c328", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>9</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e67f"}, {"id": "7d6e41", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>5</mn><mn>9</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e680"}, {"id": "75af6a", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e681"}, {"id": "b2869b", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>7</mn><mn>9</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e682"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e683", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b6b"], "concepts": ["60e2fb9d66f9ce3f243b52bb"], "question": {"text": "The gravitational potential at a point above the surface of earth is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>-</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>7</mn></msup><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi><mo> </mo><msup><mi>kg</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> and the acceleration due to gravity at that point is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>4</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo> </mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math>. Assume that the mean radius of earth to be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6400</mn><mo> </mo><mi>km</mi></math>. The height of this point above the earth's surface is:"}, "options": [{"id": "8e5c54", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1600</mn><mo> </mo><mi>km</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e684"}, {"id": "5fb99c", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>540</mn><mo> </mo><mi>km</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e685"}, {"id": "b53f3e", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1200</mn><mo> </mo><mi>km</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e686"}, {"id": "74a5d5", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1000</mn><mo> </mo><mi>km</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e687"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e688", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e5671c499d1e49588f87fd"], "concepts": ["60e56812499d1e49588fa38a"], "question": {"text": "A satellite is launched into a circular orbit of radius ' R ' around the earth. A second satellite is launched into an orbit of radius 1.03 R . The time period of revolution of the second satellite is larger than the first one approximately by"}, "options": [{"id": "d7ec4b", "text": "$9 \\%$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e689"}, {"id": "3825bf", "text": "$3 \\%$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e68a"}, {"id": "2b338c", "text": "$4.5 \\%$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e68b"}, {"id": "3cff50", "text": "$2.5 \\%$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e68c"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e68d", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["6117b60a478ae845c43c63f1"], "concepts": ["6117b8bd67dbe2603c3e85a2"], "question": {"text": "The masses and radii of the earth and moon are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced></math> respectively. Their centres are at a distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi></math>&nbsp;apart. Find the minimum escape velocity for a particle of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi></math> &nbsp;to be projected from the middle of these two masses :"}, "options": [{"id": "a14383", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>G</mi><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mi>r</mi></mfrac></msqrt></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e68e"}, {"id": "9071d7", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>G</mi><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mi>r</mi></mfrac></msqrt></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e68f"}, {"id": "9b4674", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>G</mi><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mi>r</mi></mfrac></msqrt></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e690"}, {"id": "56b322", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn><mi>G</mi></msqrt><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mi>r</mi></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e691"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e692", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f3e"], "concepts": ["6162faee6ed13040ec849245"], "question": {"text": "If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>V</mi></math> is the gravitational potential due to sphere of uniform density on its surface, then its value at the centre of sphere will be:"}, "options": [{"id": "f63b58", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>V</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e693"}, {"id": "5b55d4", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e694"}, {"id": "119f64", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>V</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e695"}, {"id": "2e5ebc", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>V</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e696"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f902ee0e06