row><mn>9</mn></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6a5"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6a6", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f3e"], "concepts": ["6162faee6ed13040ec849243"], "question": {"text": "Four identical particles of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi></math> are kept at the four corners of a square. If the gravitational force exerted on one of the masses by the other masses is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>32</mn></mfrac></mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mi>G</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></math>, the length of the sides of the square is"}, "options": [{"id": "27874a", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6a7"}, {"id": "20e852", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mi>L</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6a8"}, {"id": "596e7d", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mi>L</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6a9"}, {"id": "d988b3", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6aa"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6ab", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e5678e499d1e49588f927a"], "concepts": ["60e5678c499d1e49588f9251"], "question": {"text": "<p>A body weighs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>49</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math> on a spring balance at the north pole. What will be its weight recorded on the same weighing machine, if it is shifted to the equator?</p><p>[Use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>G</mi><mi>M</mi></mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math>&nbsp;and radius of earth, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6400</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>km</mi><mo>.</mo></math>]</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "a227d2", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>49</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>17</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6ac"}, {"id": "449607", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>48</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>83</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6ad"}, {"id": "a28629", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>49</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>83</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6ae"}, {"id": "8f4ce5", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>49</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6af"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6b0", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b6b"], "concepts": ["60e56768499d1e49588f8ed7"], "question": {"text": "Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion $\\mathrm{A}$ and the other is labelled as Reason $\\mathrm{R}$ Assertion A : The kinetic energy needed to project a body of mass m from earth surface to infinity is $\\frac{1}{2} \\mathrm{mgR}$, where R is the radius of earth.<br/>Reason $\\mathrm{R}$ : The maximum potential energy of a body is zero when it is projected to infinity from earth surface.<br/>In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the option given below"}, "options": [{"id": "28e4c8", "text": "$\\mathrm{A}$ False but $\\mathrm{R}$ is true", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6b1"}, {"id": "93122c", "text": "Both $\\mathrm{A}$ and $\\mathrm{R}$ are true and $\\mathrm{R}$ is the correct explanation of $\\mathrm{A}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6b2"}, {"id": "30c9b3", "text": "$\\mathrm{A}$ is true but $\\mathrm{R}$ is false", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6b3"}, {"id": "01e202", "text": "(4) Both $\\mathrm{A}$ and $\\mathrm{R}$ are true but $\\mathrm{R}$ is NOT the correct explanation of $\\mathrm{A}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6b4"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6b5", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4763"], "concepts": ["60e40fd4f1ef260f84ed1e41"], "question": {"text": "The time period of a satellite revolving around earth in a given orbit is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>7</mn></math> hours. If the radius of orbit is increased to three times its previous value, then approximate new time period of the satellite will be"}, "options": [{"id": "074ecd", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>36</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>hours</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6b6"}, {"id": "e3c5d2", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>40</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>hours</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6b7"}, {"id": "d5ca33", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>30</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>hours</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6b8"}, {"id": "001486", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>25</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>hours</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6b9"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6ba", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["6117b60a478ae845c43c63f1"], "concepts": ["617aa9c2ef3fe65ad8e813c5"], "question": {"text": "Four identical particles of mass <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi></math> are located at the corners of a square of side <math><mi>&#8216;</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>&#8217;</mi></math> . What should be their speed if each of them revolves under the influence of other&rsquo;s gravitational field in a circular orbit circumscribing the square?<br /><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F3f08c95f-95ed-400e-aa98-d6c0076372f7-image2.png?alt=media\" />"}, "options": [{"id": "77f042", "text": "<math><mn>1.35</mn><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6bb"}, {"id": "fce37a", "text": "<math><mi> </mi><mn>1.21</mn><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6bc"}, {"id": "874120", "text": "<math><mn>1.41</mn><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6bd"}, {"id": "55c20a", "text": "<math><mi> </mi><mn>1.16</mn><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6be"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6bf", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e5678e499d1e49588f927a"], "concepts": ["6162faf16ed13040ec849361"], "question": {"text": "The height &lsquo;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></math>&rsquo; at which the weight of a body will be the same as that at the same depth &lsquo;h&rsquo; from the surface of the earth is (Radius of the earth is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></math> and effect of the rotation of the earth is neglected)&nbsp;"}, "options": [{"id": "92f60b", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6c0"}, {"id": "64970c", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6c1"}, {"id": "72aeb2", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6c2"}, {"id": "745be8", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6c3"}], "questionLabels": []}], "markingScheme": {"_id": "608283e223cf4b3ad8f125e6", "title": "JEE Single Correct", "correctAttempt": 4, "incorrectAttempt": -1, "createdAt": "2021-04-09T08:45:27.797Z", "updatedAt": "2021-07-05T05:39:15.785Z", "__v": 0, "updated_by": "6042252509c5bf34e8a2f178"}, "maximumAttemptLimit": 20, "linkedQuestions": [], "createdAt": "2026-04-14T14:30:40.384Z", "updatedAt": "2026-04-14T14:30:40.384Z", "__v": 0}, "position": 0, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e70b"}, {"sectionId": {"_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e709", "title": "Physics Numerical", "sectionType": "normal", "subject": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "questions": [{"_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6ec", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b6b"], "concepts": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b2f"], "question": {"text": "The radius in kilometer to which the present radius of earth <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6400</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>km</mi><mo>)</mo></math> to be compressed so that the escape velocity is increased <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn></math> time is _______."}, "options": [{"id": "49939e", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6ed"}, {"id": "6f5c16", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6ee"}, {"id": "746510", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6ef"}, {"id": "04e2d0", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6f0"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6f1", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e5671c499d1e49588f87fd"], "concepts": ["60e5671a499d1e49588f87c5"], "question": {"text": "Two satellites <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> are revolving in circular orbits around a planet with radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3200</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>km</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>800</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>km</mi></math> respectively. The ratio of speed of satellite <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> to the speed of satellite <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> in their respective orbits would be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>x</mi></mfrac></math> where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo></math>"}, "options": [{"id": "9e458c", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6f2"}, {"id": "da670e", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6f3"}, {"id": "49e63d", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6f4"}, {"id": "e5efbd", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6f5"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6f6", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f3e"], "concepts": ["6162faee6ed13040ec849243"], "question": {"text": "Acceleration due to gravity on the surface of earth is ' $g$ '. If the diameter of earth is reduced to one third of its original value and mass remains unchanged, then the acceleration due to gravity on the surface of the earth is $\\ldots\\ldots$ g."}, "options": [{"id": "e262ac", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6f7"}, {"id": "764002", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6f8"}, {"id": "b4a1a3", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6f9"}, {"id": "b7afc2", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6fa"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6fb", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e5678e499d1e49588f927a"], "concepts": ["60e567fa499d1e49588fa01b"], "question": {"text": "<p>In the reported figure of earth, the value of acceleration due to gravity is same at point&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>C</mi></math>&nbsp;but it is smaller than that of its value at point&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math> (surface of the earth). The value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>O</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></math>&nbsp;will be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>:</mo><mn>5</mn></math>. The value of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math> is</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F1e4d0b7b-cbfa-4902-a9ff-047584631b2a-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 152px; height: 204px;\" /></p>"}, "options": [{"id": "4689eb", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6fc"}, {"id": "f095d0", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6fd"}, {"id": "f7da73", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6fe"}, {"id": "b3061f", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e6ff"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e700", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4763"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4719"], "question": {"text": "Two planets, $A$ and $B$ are orbiting a common star in circular orbits of radii $R_A$ and $R_B$, respectively, with $R_B=2 R_A$. The planet $B$ is $4 \\sqrt{2}$ times more massive than planet $A$. The ratio $\\left(\\frac{L_B}{L_A}\\right)$ of angular momentum $\\left(L_B\\right)$ of planet $B$ to that of planet $A\\left(L_A\\right)$ is closest to integer ________."}, "options": [{"id": "3eba19", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e701"}, {"id": "69398e", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e702"}, {"id": "a4f393", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f902ee0e063d929e703"},